<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro" name="rplidar_a1">

    <!--
      RPLidar A1 (A1M8) accessory for the CIRCE rover.

      The visual is meshes/rplidar_a1.stl, a manufacturer CAD model of the A1M8.
      Collision stays a PRIMITIVE box - see the note on the collision element below.

      *** THE MESH CONFIRMED a1_scan_plane_z AND CORRECTED a1_total_height. ***
      Measured off the CAD: the turret's spin axis is fixed to 0.04 mm by fitting the
      two complete circles it presents (the solid top cap and the turret floor), which
      agree with each other to dx=0.003 dz=0.042 mm, and the cap measures 70.00 mm
      across - the datasheet width exactly. The transparent window band runs y=34..49
      in mesh coordinates, so the optical midline - the scan plane - is at y=41.5.

      That puts the scan plane 41.5 mm above the bottom of the unit, against the 41.0
      assumed here: agreement to 0.5 mm, from an independent source. This is the number
      that matters, because lidar_mount_z, the 0.295 m scan height and
      circe_perception's scan_pitch_filter lidar_height all hang off it.

      a1_total_height did NOT survive: the CAD is 51.0 mm tall, not 60.0, and every bit
      of the 9 mm sits ABOVE the scan plane (9.5 mm modelled, 19.0 assumed). It is left
      at 0.060 deliberately - see the warning on that property.

      Datasheet figures used (RPLIDAR A1M8):
        body        98.5 x 70.0 x 60.0 mm
        range       0.15 - 12.0 m, 360 deg
        scan rate   5.5 Hz nominal, up to 10 Hz

      *** MEASURE BEFORE DRIVING ***
      lidar_mount_* below are the offsets from chassis_link to the A1's scan plane.
      x/y are carried over from the S2 mount point; z is an estimate. A wrong z puts
      obstacles at the wrong height in the costmap, and a wrong yaw makes the whole
      map rotate relative to the robot. Verify all four against the real build.

      Parented to chassis_link, not payload_link: CIRCE does not use the
      rover_dev_payload deck.

      lidar_mount_z is set on a RISER above the chassis top deck, not resting on it.
      The deck top (flipper_chassis.dae) is at z = +0.0630 in chassis_link, so a
      value of 0.170 puts the A1's base plate 0.170 - 0.041 = 0.129 up, i.e. a
      66 mm standoff above the deck.

      This clears the wheels, which was the point. The tyres reach 0.252 above
      ground (radius 0.1266 on a 0.125 axle) and the tracks reach 0.203; resting the
      A1 straight on the deck would have put the scan plane at 0.229, BELOW the
      tyre tops, so all four wheels would have shown up as permanent returns in
      fixed directions and wrecked scan matching. At 0.170 the scan plane is 0.295
      above ground, clearing the tyres by 43 mm.

      circe_sensor_mast.urdf.xacro derives the camera shelf height from
      lidar_mount_* and a1_scan_plane_z, so changing the values here moves the
      shelf too.

      Driver-side params for this sensor live in
      circe_bringup/config/lidar_a1.yaml; frame_id there must stay "lidar_link".

      NOTE: this is now a hardcoded height, no longer chassis_top_z +
      a1_scan_plane_z. If the deck height or the A1's internal geometry is
      remeasured, this value does not follow automatically, so recheck the standoff.
    -->
    <!-- Height of the scan plane above the A1's own base plate (approx). -->
    <xacro:property name="a1_scan_plane_z" value="0.041" />
    <xacro:property name="a1_base_height" value="0.020" />

    <!-- Top face of flipper_chassis.dae, measured from the mesh, in chassis_link. -->
    <xacro:property name="chassis_top_z" value="0.0630" />
    <xacro:property name="lidar_mount_x" value="0.18" />
    <xacro:property name="lidar_mount_y" value="0.0" />
    <xacro:property name="lidar_mount_z" value="0.170" />
    <!--
      *** lidar_mount_yaw IS NOW LOAD-BEARING OUTSIDE THIS FILE. ***
      circe_perception/config/scan_sector_filter.yaml masks the rear half of the sweep
      by BEARING IN lidar_link, and it is this 180 deg yaw that makes the rover's rear
      sit at bearing 0 and its front at +/-180. Change this value and that mask rotates
      with it - at yaw 0 the same config would blind the rover forwards and keep the
      cable spool. Change both together, then re-check in RViz that the blanked arc is
      behind the robot.
    -->
    <xacro:property name="lidar_mount_yaw" value="3.1415" />
    <!-- base + rotor must total the datasheet's 60 mm so the modelled top matches
         the collision box and the shelf clearance computed by the mast. -->
    <xacro:property name="a1_rotor_height" value="0.040" />
    <xacro:property name="a1_rotor_radius" value="0.0355" />

    <!--
      ===================== MESH PLACEMENT =====================
      meshes/rplidar_a1.stl is the manufacturer CAD, in MILLIMETRES, with +Y as the
      spin axis and +X/+Z horizontal. rpy=(pi/2,0,0) maps mesh (x,y,z) -> ros (x,-z,y),
      i.e. mesh +Y becomes ROS +Z (up) while mesh +X is left as ROS +X.

      a1_mesh_xyz is -R * (37.101, 41.5, 36.518) mm: the point on the turret's spin
      axis at the scan plane. lidar_link IS the scan plane, so aligning the mesh's own
      optical midline to this link's origin is the physically meaningful fit and it
      needs no reference to the A1's ambiguous "base plate" datum. The mesh's bottom
      then lands at 0.1285 in chassis_link, against the 0.129 this file's header
      derives independently - a 0.5 mm cross-check that the placement is right.

      *** a1_mesh_yaw IS COSMETIC. ***
      It spins the drawn body about the scan axis; it cannot move the scan plane and it
      cannot affect /scan, whose bearings come from the driver. It exists because the
      CAD gives no marking for the A1's own 0 deg reference, so which way the motor
      bulge points in RViz is a guess. Set it once you can see the real unit next to the
      rendered one. Do NOT reach for it to fix a rotated map - that is lidar_mount_yaw,
      and it is a different problem with real consequences.
    -->
    <xacro:property name="a1_mesh_xyz" value="-0.037101 0.036518 -0.0415" />
    <xacro:property name="a1_mesh_yaw" value="0" />

    <!--
      lidar_link sits AT the scan plane, because that is the origin the driver
      stamps /scan with. Visuals are pushed downward from it to sit on the mount.
    -->
    <link name="lidar_link">
        <visual>
            <origin xyz="${a1_mesh_xyz}" rpy="1.5707963 0 ${a1_mesh_yaw}"/>
            <geometry>
                <mesh filename="package://circe_description/meshes/rplidar_a1.stl"
                      scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
            </geometry>
        </visual>

        <!--
          Collision is deliberately still the datasheet BOX, not the mesh.

          The box is what every measurement in this workspace was taken against - the
          spool's blind sector, the mast clearance, the costmap footprint - and swapping
          in a concave 35 k-triangle hull would invalidate them for no navigational gain:
          nothing ever collides with the lidar, it sits in the middle of the robot. A
          mesh collision here would also cost gz-sim's contact solver real time every
          step. Visual fidelity and collision cheapness are separate concerns; this file
          takes the mesh for one and keeps the primitive for the other.
        -->
        <collision>
            <origin xyz="0 0 ${-a1_scan_plane_z + 0.030}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <box size="0.0985 0.070 0.060"/>
            </geometry>
        </collision>

        <inertial>
            <origin xyz="0 0 ${-a1_scan_plane_z + 0.030}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <mass value="0.170" />
            <inertia ixx="7.0e-5" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="9.0e-5" iyz="0.0" izz="1.1e-4" />
        </inertial>
    </link>

    <joint name="lidar_to_chassis" type="fixed">
        <parent link="chassis_link"/>
        <child link="lidar_link"/>
        <origin xyz="${lidar_mount_x} ${lidar_mount_y} ${lidar_mount_z}"
                rpy="0 0 ${lidar_mount_yaw}"/>
    </joint>

    <!--
      Simulated A1. Sim only - see the sim_mode note in circe_rover.urdf.xacro.

      The Sensors system that renders this used to be declared below, inside the model.
      It is world-scoped in Harmonic and now lives in circe_gazebo/worlds/*.sdf; a
      gpu_lidar in a world without it produces no /scan at all.
    -->
    <xacro:arg name="sim_mode" default="false" />
    <xacro:if value="$(arg sim_mode)">
    <gazebo reference="lidar_link">
        <sensor name='gpu_lidar' type='gpu_lidar'>
            <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
            <topic>scan</topic>
            <!-- gz_frame_id: ignition_frame_id is the Fortress spelling. -->
            <gz_frame_id>lidar_link</gz_frame_id>
            <update_rate>10</update_rate>
            <lidar>
                <scan>
                <horizontal>
                    <!-- ~4000 samples/s at 5.5 Hz standard mode -> ~720 points/rev -->
                    <samples>720</samples>
                    <resolution>1</resolution>
                    <min_angle>-3.14159</min_angle>
                    <max_angle>3.14159</max_angle>
                </horizontal>
                <vertical>
                    <samples>1</samples>
                    <resolution>1</resolution>
                    <min_angle>0.0</min_angle>
                    <max_angle>0.0</max_angle>
                </vertical>
                </scan>
                <range>
                <min>0.15</min>
                <max>12.0</max>
                <resolution>0.01</resolution>
                </range>
                <!--
                  A real A1 is noisy; a noiseless sim scan makes scan matching look
                  better than it will be on hardware. 0.01 m sigma is the A1's quoted
                  accuracy.

                  Must sit inside <lidar>, not <sensor>. SDF silently accepts an
                  unknown child of <sensor> and only mutters "XML Element[noise] ...
                  not defined in SDF. Copying as children" during load, so a
                  misplaced noise block applies nothing at all and looks like it
                  worked.
                -->
                <noise>
                    <type>gaussian</type>
                    <mean>0.0</mean>
                    <stddev>0.01</stddev>
                </noise>
            </lidar>
            <visualize>true</visualize>
        </sensor>
    </gazebo>
    </xacro:if>

</robot>
